"""Book 18 (The Interview Book) chapter briefs: data for tools/make_briefs.py --book 18.

Shape (user ruling 2026-09-28; sources/BATCH_BOOKS_17-18.md, WRITING_A_QUANT_BOOK.md section 5, "Book 18
anatomy"). New questions only: nothing is reprinted, harvested or indexed from Books 1-17. Each chapter is the
hook, a short method lesson (2-4 pp: the families of questions, the method for each, worked `example` boxes),
ONE bank of about 12-15 `interviewq` ramped from one star to three, each tagged `\\iqroles{...}` (trader,
researcher, developer, mle, bank, risk) and `\\iqfirm{...}` (market maker, proprietary firm, multi-manager fund,
systematic fund, bank, asset manager, crypto firm, any), then `omsources`. No exercises, weekend problem,
tutorial or build (ch. 21 may keep a short tutorial). Part I and ch. 28 carry 8-12 questions; ch. 29 is six
transcribed mock interviews whose questions are `interviewq` inside the transcripts. Every solution ends with
`\\iqlookfor{...}`. Budget ~8 pp a chapter all-in (~300 body lines, ~200 solution lines), ch. 29 ~18 pp.

`uses` lists terms defined elsewhere that the chapter relies on (not redefined); owners in parentheses:
(B4.12) = One Quant Book 4, chapter 12, checked against the multi-line \\index{} harvest of Books 1-16 on
2026-09-28 (3,383 definitions); (B17.13) = Book 17, written in this batch, by its sync-time map sources/industry/DEFINITIONS.md; (ch6) = a chapter of this book. `defines` holds interview-process and question-method
terms only: mathematics, markets, ML and systems vocabulary belongs to Books 1-16 and is used with prose
pointers ("One Quant Book 4, chapter 12"). Book 16 owns the compensation-policy and restrictive-covenant terms
ch. 7 uses; Book 17 owns the roles (ch. 1), pay structure and pay data (ch. 7), career paths and competitions
(ch. 3).

Bank field: families, planned count and star split (one/two/three stars), role mix, firm-type mix.
Wording: original. Each question is written from its family's idea in a new setting with new numbers; a
classic puzzle appears only as a variant whose answer differs from the folklore version, and its known origin
(the first publication, never an interview book or website) is cited in `omsources`. No question is ever
attributed to a firm. An originality test (code/interviews/originality/) asserts that no 8-word shingle of a
Book 18 question or solution appears in any `interviewq`, `exercise` or `problem` of Books 1-17.

Tests (code/interviews/NN-slug/tests/test_solutions.py, teaching modules prefixed `iv_`): exact answers by
fractions.Fraction, sympy or exhaustive enumeration; probabilistic answers also by simulation over many seeds
(fast set < 20 s; full-size runs `@pytest.mark.reference`); coding answers are real files tested against a
brute-force oracle on random inputs (Python), `*_test.cpp` built by tools/test_code.sh with -std=c++20 -Wall
-Wextra -Werror (C++), a Cargo crate with no dependencies under clippy -D warnings (Rust); output-prediction
questions are compiled/run by pytest and their stdout asserted (at -O0 and -O2, g++ and the pinned rustc);
undefined behaviour is stated in the solution, never "tested": the snippet is built under
-fsanitize=address,undefined and the test asserts the sanitizer's diagnosis, no output; implementation-defined
results (sizeof, alignment) are asserted for x86-64 Linux and the solution says so; Rust compile-fail answers
invoke rustc on the snippet and assert the error code; SQL answers are .sql files run by DuckDB (and Python's
sqlite3 where the dialect allows) on generated fixtures and compared with a pandas implementation; Fermi
answers assert the chain's arithmetic and that the ledgered reference value lies in the printed interval.

No running-project components: answer checkers live under code/interviews/. No measured timings.
"""
